   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">During the reporting period, the Government of Indonesia (GoI) has revitalised Governmental One-Roof Integrated Services (LTSA) program to provide improved services to migrant workers and better protection of their rights as mandated by Law No. 18/2017. To date, GoI has established 45 LTSAs in the country. In June 2021, GoI has started the process of reintegrating Migrant Resources Center (MRC) services in the LTSA aimed at providing more integrated services to migrant workers such as pre-employment consultation, safe and fair migration information, complaint mechanism and counselling. Additionally, GoI has integrated village-based protection with LTSA in cooperation with trade unions and women crisis centers aimed at addressing violence and harassment particularly toward women migrant workers. The LTSAs are financed by the government which ensures the program sustainability in the future. With support from the European Union, the ILO has supported the integration of gender-responsive Migrant Worker Resources Centers (MRCs) within the One-Roof Integrated Service Office (LTSA)) and local government units in four districts in Indonesia i.e. East Lampung, Cirebon, Blitar and Tulunagung. The ILO has supported the piloting of multi-stakeholder partnership among government, trade unions and civil society-managed women crisis centers in order to provide integrated and coordinated strategies to improve access to services and rights protection of women migrant workers and their families at every stage of migration. Through the MRCs, ILO has piloted village-based protection networks in 36 villages to promote safe and fair migration and the provision of services with particular focus on addressing specific challenges faced by women migrant workers, and issues of labour exploitation, abuse, violence, harassment, and trafficking in persons.
With the support from ILO RBTC, the ILO has supported the GoI&apos;s social assistance program to migrant workers affected by COVID-19 through entrepreneurship training.</narrative>
   </description>
