  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">the Government of Indonesia (GoI) has issued new regulations aimed at improving labour migration governance and migrant workers&apos; rights. The regulations include Government Regulation No. 10/2020 on Procedures for Placement of Migrant Workers in January 2020; Regulation of Protection Board (PERBAN) No. 09/2020 on Zero Placement Fee for Indonesian Migrant Workers in July 2020; Head of Protection Board Decree (PERKA) No. 323/2020 on Guidelines on Zero Placement Fee Implementation in December 2020; Government Regulation (PP) No. 59/2021 on Implementation of Protection of Indonesian Migrant Workers in April 2021 as the implementing regulation to Law No. 18/2017 on Indonesian Migrant Workers Protection. The GoI has issued a new policy to response COVID-19 situation through the issuance of Ministerial Decree No. 294/2020 on the Placement of Indonesian Migrant Workers under the new normal, adopting the ILO Guidelines on Safe Return to Work. With support from the European Union, the ILO has supported the implementation of Law 18/2017 on the Protection of Indonesian Migrants and technical assistance on the development of its implementing regulation (PP No. 59/2021) through research, tripartite dialogues, workshop to formulate a set of recommendations during the regulation drafting process.  The ILO also facilitated a tripartite dialogue which resulted in the recommendation for a zero recruitment cost policy, which was submitted to the GoI. The ILO also has provided technical assistance to GoI in drafting the guidelines on safe return to work for migrant workers in a pandemic COVID-19 situation, including standard operational procedures for Training Centers (BLKs) and private recruitment agencies to help better protect migrant workers. 
The ILO has supported the deliberation of domestic workers bill in the parliament and facilitated some national dialogues with tripartite constituents and civil society. The Bill is listed in the parliament&apos;s legislation priority program (PROLEGNAS) this year.</narrative>
   </description>
